hink there's  often a tendency for us in database  to see our job as just making a thing  and then we put the thing out there and  we forget about it  but really really if there's one thing i  can get to you  from today is don't do that stick around  enjoy the chat  the next point um big big learning curve  for me  and again that audience feedback was  absolutely critical to this was  the risk of trying to be too clever  trying to be too technical with  solutions to  database problems and essentially losing  people along the way and losing trust  along the way so the term blinding with  science i'm sure most of you familiar  with but for those who aren't it's the  idea that you  you're telling someone about something  in an overly complex or technical way  so that regardless of the the result of  what you've done  they end up going away scratching their  head they're not quite sure  because of the terminology because of  the methodology they're not quite sure  what you've done  and i think there's often a risk with  that in database so  the question that we had um this was  still back in the spring  was could we improve the way we drew the  lines on our charts  so that instead of using a simple seven  day rolling average as we had been doing  we got slightly smoother lines but we  also got lines where the final data  point on the chart was a  more faithful reflection of the final  data point in real life  because of course the problem when  you're doing a seven day average whether  you  align that seven day average to the  right to the middle to the left  is it's never going to be a especially  close representation of the last  data point you have because it's being  influenced by the other six  and so the issue we were having is the  numbers  in our headlines in our news stories at  the ft would always be referring to that  last data point the thing that has just  happened  but our charts were referring to the  seven day average which was  while cases were going up always lower  than that and so we would have this  tension where  the numbers on our charts didn't quite  reflect the numbers we used  so what we decided to do was look at  whether we could optimize the way we  actually draw the lines on the chart so  that they're still smooth  as you get with an average but they also  actually  more faithfully reflect the very latest  data point you have  so for those of you who have only ever  seen seven day average charts of covert  data  this is what the underlying data  actually looks like it's incredibly  noisy you get these  huge peaks and troughs um new daily  cases will  drop to zero and then shoot up to a  thousand the next day because  the there are the way that this data is  actually collected and reported  leads to enormous um differences  in the numbers you get from day to day  there's weekly cyclicality there's all  sorts of stuff going on  so we definitely didn't want the data to  look like this now  of course the last point of each line  here would reflect reality would reflect  the last data point we had  but it's far too noisy we can publish  that so here's the rolling average  method we have been using  much clearer much smoother you can see  exactly what's going on you get  clear trends over time you can see here  for example that italy had  reached its peak and started coming back  down but  the last data point on each of these  lines didn't necessarily match up with  the last data point that had been  recorded  so what we considered was a smoothing  spline  so it keeps that smoothness of the lines  it's still relatively easy to discern  what's going on in one country  and compare it to another but the last  data point is now  because of how the splines are  calculated much closer to the last data  point that have been recorded  and you can see that in this next chart  so we actually measured  how close to the final  reported data point was the last data  point of each line  when we use this method on the left hand  side you've got a simple rolling average  on the right hand side you've got the  spline  and you can see that the data points are  much closer to the real reported value  on the right  so we decided let's switch over to the  spline  and we did again a long explainer  of why we've done this um setting out  that need to  to better represent the most recent data  while keeping smoothness  big explanation out in the public for  anyone to see  and i then again following my own advice  ask people what do they think  and here's the kind of responses we got  and what you'll notice is there were  sort of two  quite clearly different camps here on  the left  top left we've got a couple of people  saying spline's much better i  yeah i'm very devoted to spoiling myself  for example  and the thing i should point out  immediately about that is that  these two people peter and mike and i  don't know if any either of them are  actually  um watching along today are both um  data scientists to some degree so you  know i think anyone who's heard of  splines was  very i felt very positive about that as  an idea  um but then we get some different  feedback in the upper right we've got  someone saying the rolling average is  much easier for the average person to  understand  and in the lower left we've got someone  with a data science background but still  saying that the rolling average  probably makes more sense and then in  the lower left is we actually have one  of the epidemiologists in the uk  working on the development of the  vaccines  talking about how this is for the  general public  these the audience for this work is a  mass audience it's a very  an audience with very mixed visual and  data literacy and it's much easier for  people to get  their head around a seven day average  than a spline  so different people different with  different um personal contexts  had very different views on this so  the takeaway that we had again from this  was that you have to prioritize the ease  of understanding you may have come up  with an incredibly  technical nifty solution that on the  basis of any sort of quantitative  tests works better but if that is going  to confuse people along the way  that is a problem and again for me the  fundamental goal of my data  visualizations is communication with a  mass audience  and i would rather have clear  communication  and as many people as possible  understanding  remembering and wanting to share these  charts than to have something which is  five percent more uh correct  shall we say but that loses a lot of  people along the way  the final lesson that we've learned just  in the last couple of weeks i think in  our  work on the pandemic has been the  importance  and just the way that you can use  animation  to tell a story much much more  effectively than telling that same story  in a static  chart and so i've done  i've done a couple of bits of animation  over the last couple of years which have  been especially effective  and i think the lesson i've really  learned from this is that it's  the the sense of tension and of  anticipation that you can build up  when you do animation well um  can produce a much much stronger  response from people a much stronger  reaction  than doing exactly the same chart as a  static chart so i'm going to show you  two examples of that and there are  examples that some of you may have seen  in recent days which are where i wanted  to make the point  that covid really is considerably worse  for example  than a bad flu so here's the first chart  and what we're looking at here this  again this is a gif it's an animation  it's going to loop so if you miss it the  first time we'll see it the second time  we're looking at the weekly rate of  people admitted into icu so intensive  care units  in england with flu in a typical winter  over the last few years and then  contrasting that with what we've seen  this year for covid  and you'll see quite clearly that fluid  missions in the past were  even when we thought they were high so  even when we had what at the time were  seen as record bad  flu winters those figures were much  much lower than what we've seen over the  last month or two  in england with covid so this was really  attempt to  put to bed this myth this idea that oh  kovid's just  you know it just flew rebranded it's a  new version of what we've seen before  and show that this really is a  completely different beast  now some people would come back to this  and say well you know we're just testing  a lot more for kobe this year  so that's why we see the numbers we see  much higher numbers than we usually  would with flu  but again i had another animated  response to that which is to show the  total numbers of people in icu  in intensive care in london london uk  for any reason and compare that again  from winter to winter  so in a typical year even during a bad  flu season  the numbers of people in intensive care  in london never go above about 750  so again this is whether they're in  there for flu whether they're in there  for a heart attack for a stroke any any  of that but the numbers  of people in there for all causes never  really go above about 750 and are  typically around 700  whereas this year they've gone up above  1200 and in fact in recent days have  gone closer to 1400  so almost double the number of people in  intensive care in london this year than  a usual year  and unless there's been a sudden surge  in the numbers of people  falling down multiple flights of stairs  we can point to the one thing that is  different about this year which is coded  but the point is for a what i consider  to be a really important  topic here which is correcting  relatively widely held myths  that what we were seeing this winter was  nothing out of the ordinary and  therefore  we don't need to be in lockdown we don't  need any restrictions because this is  just like any other winter  you gain an enormous amount by producing  this as an animation  it's it's a much more engaging piece of  work  and this graphic on twitter alone has  now been seen  by more than 10 million people which is  far more people have seen this graphic  than any other chart that we produce at  the ft  all year on social media and that i  think  and in fact it's not just what i think a  lot of the responses on social media  have explicitly  pointed to this the use of animation was  enormously  was pivotal to that in terms of how it  grabs attention more than a static chart  in terms of how it builds suspense and  in terms of how you get that shock that  surprise factor at the end when you  compare the two  so the key point there being where it  where it's suitable  animation is an incredibly powerful  technique to use to convey a message now  i think it should be used sparingly if  you suddenly make everything animated  it becomes it's like the the old days of  90s internet where you've got pop-ups  everywhere um  everything's too noisy it's hard to  actually get across your key point and  everything gets drowned out  but using animation sparingly i think is  an incredibly powerful technique  so all of that is to say that the last  12 months i think have been  an incredible crash course for me and  for our team at the ft  in terms of really getting to the  fundamentals of how people do  read charts and therefore what we should  be thinking about when we make charts  what we should be focusing on  prioritizing  and coming into this year i think  my assumption and a lot of people here  i'm sure the assumption would have been  that  people read charts a bit like this they  get out their protractor their set  square their ruler  and their their compass and they it's  all about precision and  people are really focusing on individual  pixels and  and where different elements are in your  plot when they're trying to take away  their message they're  they're almost trying to pass the data  set back out of your chart and so  you as the practitioner should really be  focusing on the most high fidelity  most clear encoding of that data as  possible  um but the reality i think is actually a  bit more like this  it's a bit more like people going to a  movie theater and sitting back and  watching  they are coming to a chart to  see something to see some kind of story  to see  to get a message to to take away  something that is playing out in front  of them  you know in some cases literally with  the animation but in other cases it's  the line chart  but it's a line chart where they're not  focusing on  you know tiny differences between data  points on the chart they're focusing on  what is this top line message in the  title what's the takeaway here  and what are the other sort of sweeping  trends that this chart is conveying  they're not poring over it trying to  extract the values from every pixel on  the page  so to summarize the key lessons i think  i've learned here and i  i think these are lessons that i'm going  to be using for the rest of my my career  um first of all text is critical i know  we're all in the  database industry we make charts but if  we don't get the text right  none of the rest really matters people  will be lost they'll be confused  it will all be a disaster the second  point we have to take responsibility  if someone is confused about our chart  that's our fault and the best thing we  can do to combat that is to include  explanations um in situ  around where that chart appears to make  sure that there's as little risk as  possible confusion  on a similar note we need to be  considering  what are people's beliefs feelings  emotions sentiments around this subject  matter and how could they color their  reaction to this chart  how can we design this chart in as  neutral a way as possible in that sense  so that at least if anyone does have any  issues with their chart it's because  they don't like the data  it's not because they feel put off  or hurt somehow by the way we've  actually conveyed the information  the next step and i think again one of  the most important don't just publish  and vanish  stick around get involved in the  conversation you will learn so much  about how people are understanding your  chart that you can  incorporate into the next one you make  and  make sure that your chart above all else  is easy to understand  it doesn't matter if you can fine tune  it to make it  two three four percent more um  more faithful to the data it doesn't  even matter i think as much as  it doesn't matter as much as we think it  does about optimizing tiny little things  about how people understand um  uncertainty or ranges or confidence  intervals for example  the key thing is that you focus on  getting across that core message and  getting it across  excessively and finally just  we should all um be aware that we have  this incredibly powerful tool in  animation  and there are plenty of charts where we  would unthinkingly just produce a static  chart  but an animation can have that much more  i would say in fact orders of magnitude  more impact  and i'm going to end with a call to  action in terms of things that i would  like us as the date of his community to  do  differently or do more or even do for  the first time  as we as we go away from this which is  that first of all  as i mentioned at the very start there's  a huge amount of um huge amount of  resources out there on  how to achieve particular technical or  visual effects in a chart but there's  not very much  on the general way of framing your work  and the language you use around it in  order to achieve the clearest possible  communication of a message so what i'd  love to see and what i hope to  participate in myself  is putting out more resources that can  help people  think of and refine this idea of data  business communication  more than database just as a technical  exercise of creating  the the most objectively good chart  possible  and secondly i'd like us to be more to  think about accessibility in the  broadest of senses when we make our  charts  because we love making charts and  because we know other people  most people on this call i'm sure on  this uh on this session  um love looking at charts it can be very  easy to get into the trap of  thinking oh what's the what's the  coolest thing i can do here that other  chart people will love  how can i push the envelope how can i  make this this chart  um you know especially exciting and  impressive to people  who like charts but that is the  tiniest tip of the iceberg and the rest  of this enormous  thing beneath the surface of the sea is  non-chart people it's people who last  looked at charts when they were age 16  17 18 and then got the hell away from  them  probably people who tried to even avoid  spreadsheets but that is an enormous  enormous audience of people who could  love charts if they just had charts that  spoke to them  and charts that didn't try to be too  smart  charts that didn't condescend to them so  the other thing i'd love us all to do is  think more about making charts  not that are graphics for graphics  editors as uh  someone once said to me but charts that  are for your mum your dad  and you know everyone else out there in  the world  so thank you very much for bearing with  me through all of this and  please do find me all your questions and  i'd also encourage you in the spirit of  what i've been saying here  do you send me a tweet or an email or  anything like that with other questions  you have if we don't get time for them  just now because i'd love to hear your  thoughts  on all of this stuff  [Music]  you

